lessons.push({
  title: "HTTP Fundamentals: Requests, Responses, Methods and Status Codes",
  minutes: 55,
  learn: ["Describe the client-server model and the request-response cycle", "Read a URL, a request, a response, headers and status codes", "Choose the right HTTP method", "Make a request with the standard library and parse URLs safely"],
  why: "Every web page, mobile app and API is HTTP underneath. Knowing the protocol makes debugging, security and API design much easier.",
  realWorld: "Opening a page, submitting a form, a mobile app loading your feed, a payment gateway calling your server: all are HTTP requests and responses.",
  simple: "A <strong>client</strong> (browser, app, script) sends a <strong>request</strong> to a <strong>server</strong>; the server sends back a <strong>response</strong> with a status code, headers and usually a body. Each request is independent: HTTP is <em>stateless</em>.",
  detail: `<h3>A request and a response</h3>
${terminal("What travels over the network (simplified)", `GET /v1/students?dept=CSE HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.example.in\nAccept: application/json\nAuthorization: Bearer <token>\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\nContent-Length: 42\n\n[{"id": 1, "name": "Asha"}]`)}
<h3>URL anatomy</h3>
<p><code>https://api.example.in:8443/v1/students?dept=CSE&amp;year=2#top</code> = scheme (<code>https</code>), host, port (443 default for https, 80 for http), path, query string (<code>key=value</code> pairs joined by <code>&amp;</code>, percent-encoded), fragment (<code>#top</code>, never sent to the server).</p>
<h3>Methods</h3>
${table(["Method", "Meaning", "Safe?", "Idempotent?", "Body?"], [["GET", "read a resource", "yes", "yes", "no"], ["POST", "create / run an action", "no", "no", "yes"], ["PUT", "replace a resource", "no", "yes", "yes"], ["PATCH", "change part of a resource", "no", "not guaranteed", "yes"], ["DELETE", "remove a resource", "no", "yes", "usually no"], ["HEAD / OPTIONS", "headers only / what is allowed (CORS)", "yes", "yes", "no"]])}
<p><strong>Safe</strong> methods do not change data. <strong>Idempotent</strong> means repeating the call has the same effect as doing it once (important for retries).</p>
<h3>Status codes</h3>
${table(["Class", "Meaning", "Common codes"], [["1xx", "informational", "101 Switching Protocols"], ["2xx", "success", "200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content"], ["3xx", "redirection", "301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified"], ["4xx", "client error", "400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 405 Method Not Allowed, 409 Conflict, 422 Unprocessable Content, 429 Too Many Requests"], ["5xx", "server error", "500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable, 504 Gateway Timeout"]])}
<p>401 means &quot;who are you?&quot; (not authenticated); 403 means &quot;I know you, but you may not&quot; (not authorised).</p>
<h3>Headers worth knowing</h3>
<p><code>Content-Type</code>, <code>Accept</code>, <code>Authorization</code>, <code>User-Agent</code>, <code>Cache-Control</code>, <code>Set-Cookie</code> / <code>Cookie</code>, <code>Location</code>, <code>Retry-After</code>. <strong>HTTPS</strong> wraps HTTP in TLS so that nobody on the network can read or change it: always use it.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `from urllib.parse import urlparse, parse_qs, urlencode\nu = urlparse(url)\nparse_qs(u.query)\nurlencode({"q": "python tips"})`, note: "Build and read URLs with urllib.parse, never by string slicing." },
  diagram: graph("Request and response", "A client sends an HTTP request over the network to a server; the server processes it, possibly using a database, and returns a response with a status code and body.", 700, 190,
    [{ id: "c", k: "proc", t: "client\n(browser / app)", x: 100, y: 95, w: 150, h: 54 }, { id: "s", k: "proc", t: "server\n(your API)", x: 360, y: 95, w: 140, h: 54 }, { id: "d", k: "io", t: "database", x: 600, y: 95, w: 130, h: 46 }],
    [{ from: "c", to: "s", label: "request" }, { from: "s", to: "d" }]),
  example: { code: `import http.client\n${SERVER}\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse, parse_qs, urlencode, quote\n\nu = urlparse("https://api.example.in:8443/v1/students?dept=CSE&year=2&year=3#top")\nprint(u.scheme, u.hostname, u.port, u.path, u.query, u.fragment)\nprint(parse_qs(u.query))\nprint(urlencode({"q": "python tips", "page": 2}), quote("a b/c"))\n\nconn = http.client.HTTPConnection("127.0.0.1", server.server_port)\nconn.request("GET", "/students?name=Asha", headers={"User-Agent": "demo"})\nresponse = conn.getresponse()\nprint(response.status, response.reason, response.getheader("Content-Type"))\nprint(json.loads(response.read()))\nconn.request("GET", "/nothing")\nresponse = conn.getresponse()\nprint(response.status, response.reason, json.loads(response.read()))\nserver.shutdown()`, out: `https api.example.in 8443 /v1/students dept=CSE&year=2&year=3 top\n{'dept': ['CSE'], 'year': ['2', '3']}\nq=python+tips&page=2 a%20b/c\n200 OK application/json\n[{'id': 1, 'name': 'Asha'}]\n404 Not Found {'error': 'not found'}` },
  lineByLine: [["urlparse(...)", "Splits the URL into scheme, host, port, path, query and fragment."], ["parse_qs", "Query values are lists because a key may repeat."], ["urlencode / quote", "Percent-encoding makes special characters safe in a URL."], ["ThreadingHTTPServer in a thread", "A real HTTP server on your own machine (port 0 means any free port)."], ["http.client", "The standard library's low-level client: request, getresponse, read."], ["404 Not Found", "The server understood the request but has no such resource."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Round trip of <code>GET /students?name=Asha</code>:", headers: ["Step", "Who", "What"], rows: [["1", "client", "sends the request line and headers"], ["2", "server", "parses the path and query, filters the list"], ["3", "server", "replies 200 with a JSON body"], ["4", "client", "reads the status, headers and body"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Interpreting status codes: what should a client do?", code: `def advice(status):\n    if 200 <= status < 300:\n        return "use the data"\n    if status in (301, 302, 307, 308):\n        return "follow the Location header"\n    if status in (401,):\n        return "log in again"\n    if status == 429 or 500 <= status < 600:\n        return "wait and retry"\n    if 400 <= status < 500:\n        return "fix the request; do not retry"\n    return "unknown"\nfor code in (200, 302, 401, 404, 429, 503):\n    print(code, advice(code))`, out: `200 use the data\n302 follow the Location header\n401 log in again\n404 fix the request; do not retry\n429 wait and retry\n503 wait and retry` },
  mistakes: ["Building URLs by string concatenation without encoding.", "Using GET for actions that change data.", "Putting secrets in the URL (they end up in logs and history).", "Confusing 401 and 403.", "Ignoring HTTPS."],
  practices: ["Use <code>urllib.parse</code> or a library for URLs.", "Return correct status codes.", "Send secrets in headers or bodies over HTTPS only."],
  interview: ["What happens when you type a URL into a browser?", "GET vs POST?", "What does idempotent mean?", "401 vs 403?"],
  practiceQs: ["What are the default ports of http and https?", "Which methods are safe?", "What is in the query string of /search?q=a+b&page=2?", "What does 204 mean?"],
  revision: ["Client sends request, server sends response.", "Methods: GET read, POST create, PUT replace, PATCH change, DELETE remove.", "Status classes 2xx/3xx/4xx/5xx.", "Stateless; use HTTPS."],
  hinglish: "Client request bhejta hai aur server response lautata hai jisme status code, headers aur body hoti hai. GET data padhta hai, POST naya banata hai, PUT badalta hai aur DELETE hataata hai. 2xx safal, 4xx client ki galti aur 5xx server ki galti hoti hai.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Which status code means the resource was created?", ["200", "201", "204", "301"], 1, "201 Created."),
    mcq("Which method is safe and idempotent?", ["POST", "GET", "PATCH", "DELETE only"], 1, "GET only reads."),
    mcq("What does 404 mean?", ["Server crashed", "Resource not found", "Not authorised", "Redirect"], 1, "The path has no resource."),
    mcq("Which code means \"you are known but not allowed\"?", ["401", "403", "404", "500"], 1, "403 Forbidden."),
    mcq("What is the default port for https?", ["80", "8080", "443", "21"], 2, "443.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Read a URL", "Read one URL. Print on separate lines: the origin as scheme://host:port (use port 443 for https and 80 for http when none is given), the path, and then every query parameter as key: values (values joined by commas, keys sorted alphabetically). Use urllib.parse.", "from urllib.parse import urlparse, parse_qs\nurl = input()\n# print origin, path and the sorted query parameters\n", `from urllib.parse import urlparse, parse_qs\nurl = input()\nu = urlparse(url)\nport = u.port or (443 if u.scheme == "https" else 80)\nprint(f"{u.scheme}://{u.hostname}:{port}")\nprint(u.path)\nfor key, values in sorted(parse_qs(u.query).items()):\n    print(f"{key}: {','.join(values)}")`, [["https://api.example.in/v1/students?dept=CSE&year=2&year=3", "https://api.example.in:443\n/v1/students\ndept: CSE\nyear: 2,3"], ["http://localhost:8000/health", "http://localhost:8000\n/health"]], [["https://api.example.in/v1/students?dept=CSE&year=2&year=3", "https://api.example.in:443\n/v1/students\ndept: CSE\nyear: 2,3"], ["http://localhost:8000/health", "http://localhost:8000\n/health"], ["http://example.com/?b=2&a=1", "http://example.com:80\n/\na: 1\nb: 2"], ["https://x.in:8443/p/q?name=a%20b", "https://x.in:8443\n/p/q\nname: a b"]])],
});

