lessons.push({
  title: "Authentication Basics, Password Hashing and JWT",
  minutes: 60,
  learn: ["Separate authentication from authorisation", "Store passwords safely with salted slow hashes", "Explain sessions versus tokens", "Create and verify a JSON Web Token (HS256) and know its pitfalls"],
  why: "Almost every API must know who is calling and what they may do. Getting this wrong leads to the most serious security incidents, so the safe patterns are worth learning exactly.",
  realWorld: "Logging in to a banking app, an API key for a developer portal, a mobile app that keeps a token after sign-in.",
  simple: "<strong>Authentication</strong> proves who you are (password, token). <strong>Authorisation</strong> decides what you may do (roles, permissions). After a successful login the server gives the client a <strong>token</strong> to send with later requests.",
  detail: `<h3>Passwords</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Never store plain passwords.</strong> Store a salted, deliberately slow hash.</li><li>Use <code>argon2</code> or <code>bcrypt</code> (third-party) or the standard library's <code>hashlib.scrypt</code> / <code>hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac</code>. Never use plain MD5 or SHA-256 for passwords.</li><li>A random <strong>salt</strong> per password defeats precomputed tables; compare with <code>hmac.compare_digest</code> (constant time).</li><li>Rate-limit login attempts; support multi-factor authentication; never reveal whether the user or the password was wrong.</li></ul>
<h3>Sessions versus tokens</h3>
${table(["", "Server-side session", "Token (JWT)"], [["state", "server stores it; cookie holds an id", "stateless: the token carries claims"], ["revoke", "delete the session", "hard before expiry (use short life + refresh tokens or a block list)"], ["scaling", "needs shared session storage", "easy across servers"], ["typical use", "traditional web apps (cookies, <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite</code>)", "APIs, mobile apps, single sign-on"]])}
<h3>JWT structure</h3>
<p>A JSON Web Token has three base64url parts joined by dots: <code>header.payload.signature</code>. The header names the algorithm (<code>{"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}</code>), the payload holds <strong>claims</strong> (<code>sub</code> subject, <code>exp</code> expiry, <code>iat</code> issued at, roles...), and the signature is <code>HMAC-SHA256(secret, header + "." + payload)</code>. The payload is <em>encoded, not encrypted</em>: anyone can read it, so never put secrets in it. The signature only proves it was not changed.</p>
<h3>JWT pitfalls</h3>
<ul><li>Always verify the signature <strong>and</strong> <code>exp</code>; reject <code>alg: none</code>; fix the expected algorithm on the server.</li><li>Keep the secret long and random and out of source code; rotate it.</li><li>Short lifetimes (minutes), refresh tokens for longer sessions.</li><li>Use HTTPS; store tokens safely (avoid <code>localStorage</code> for sensitive apps; prefer <code>HttpOnly</code> cookies).</li><li>In production use a maintained library (<code>PyJWT</code>, <code>authlib</code>) rather than your own code. The standard-library version below exists to show exactly how it works.</li></ul>
<h3>Authorisation</h3>
<p>Check permissions on the server for every request (role-based access control, ownership checks). Never trust &quot;hidden&quot; buttons or client-supplied roles. Return 401 for missing or invalid credentials and 403 for insufficient permission.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `import hashlib, hmac, os\nsalt = os.urandom(16)\ndigest = hashlib.scrypt(password.encode(), salt=salt, n=2**14, r=8, p=1)\nhmac.compare_digest(a, b)`, note: "Slow, salted hashes for passwords." },
  diagram: graph("Token-based login", "The client sends credentials; the server checks the password hash and returns a signed token; later requests carry the token in the Authorization header and the server verifies the signature and expiry.", 720, 220,
    [{ id: "c", k: "proc", t: "client", x: 80, y: 110, w: 100, h: 44 }, { id: "l", k: "io", t: "POST /login\nusername + password", x: 300, y: 50, w: 190, h: 50 }, { id: "t", k: "io", t: "token (signed)", x: 300, y: 110, w: 160, h: 40 }, { id: "a", k: "io", t: "GET /data\nAuthorization: Bearer token", x: 320, y: 175, w: 230, h: 50 }, { id: "s", k: "proc", t: "server\nverify signature + exp", x: 620, y: 110, w: 170, h: 56 }],
    [{ from: "l", to: "s" }, { from: "t", to: "c" }, { from: "a", to: "s" }]),
  example: { code: `import base64, hashlib, hmac, json\n\ndef b64(data):\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).rstrip(b"=").decode()\n\ndef unb64(text):\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64decode(text + "=" * (-len(text) % 4))\n\ndef sign(secret, payload):\n    header = b64(json.dumps({"alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT"}, separators=(",", ":")).encode())\n    body = b64(json.dumps(payload, separators=(",", ":")).encode())\n    signature = hmac.new(secret.encode(), f"{header}.{body}".encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\n    return f"{header}.{body}.{b64(signature)}"\n\ndef verify(secret, token, now):\n    try:\n        header, body, signature = token.split(".")\n        expected = hmac.new(secret.encode(), f"{header}.{body}".encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\n        if json.loads(unb64(header)).get("alg") != "HS256":\n            return "bad algorithm"\n        if not hmac.compare_digest(b64(expected), signature):\n            return "bad signature"\n        claims = json.loads(unb64(body))\n    except ValueError:\n        return "malformed"\n    return "expired" if claims.get("exp", 0) < now else claims\n\ntoken = sign("s3cret", {"sub": "asha", "role": "student", "exp": 2000})\nprint(token.count("."), len(token.split(".")[0]))\nprint(verify("s3cret", token, now=1000))\nprint(verify("s3cret", token, now=3000))\nprint(verify("wrong", token, now=1000))\nhead, body, sig = token.split(".")\nforged = b64(json.dumps({"sub": "asha", "role": "admin", "exp": 2000}, separators=(",", ":")).encode())\nprint(verify("s3cret", f"{head}.{forged}.{sig}", now=1000))\nprint(verify("s3cret", "not-a-token", now=1000))\nprint(json.loads(unb64(body)))\n\nsalt = bytes(range(16))\nstored = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac("sha256", b"pw1", salt, 200_000).hex()\nprint(len(stored), hmac.compare_digest(stored, hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac("sha256", b"pw1", salt, 200_000).hex()))\nprint(hmac.compare_digest(stored, hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac("sha256", b"pw2", salt, 200_000).hex()))`, out: `2 36\n{'sub': 'asha', 'role': 'student', 'exp': 2000}\nexpired\nbad signature\nbad signature\nmalformed\n{'sub': 'asha', 'role': 'student', 'exp': 2000}\n64 True\nFalse` },
  lineByLine: [["b64 / unb64", "URL-safe base64 without padding is how JWT parts are written."], ["hmac.new(secret, header.body, sha256)", "The signature: anyone without the secret cannot create it."], ["verify: algorithm, signature, exp", "Three separate checks in a fixed order."], ["forged payload with role admin", "Changing the payload invalidates the old signature."], ["compare_digest", "A constant-time comparison avoids timing leaks."], ["pbkdf2_hmac with many iterations", "Slow on purpose so brute force is expensive; a real app uses a random salt per user."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Verifying a token:", headers: ["Step", "Check", "On failure"], rows: [["1", "three dot-separated parts", "malformed"], ["2", "header alg is the expected one", "bad algorithm"], ["3", "recomputed signature equals the sent one", "bad signature"], ["4", "exp is in the future", "expired"], ["5", "all passed", "use the claims"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Role-based authorisation: a check applied after authentication succeeded.", code: `PERMISSIONS = {"student": {"read"}, "staff": {"read", "write"}, "admin": {"read", "write", "delete"}}\ndef can(claims, action):\n    return action in PERMISSIONS.get(claims.get("role"), set())\nfor role in ("student", "staff", "admin", "ghost"):\n    print(role, [a for a in ("read", "write", "delete") if can({"role": role}, a)])`, out: `student ['read']\nstaff ['read', 'write']\nadmin ['read', 'write', 'delete']\nghost []` },
  mistakes: ["Storing passwords in plain text or with a fast hash.", "Forgetting to verify the signature or expiry.", "Accepting <code>alg: none</code> or letting the token choose the algorithm.", "Putting secrets in the JWT payload.", "Mixing up 401 and 403.", "Rolling your own crypto in production."],
  practices: ["Use argon2 or bcrypt; a unique salt per user.", "Short-lived tokens, HTTPS everywhere.", "Check authorisation on the server for every action."],
  interview: ["Authentication vs authorisation?", "How do you store passwords?", "What is in a JWT and is it encrypted?", "Sessions vs JWT: trade-offs?"],
  practiceQs: ["Why is the JWT payload not secret?", "What does the signature protect?", "Why use compare_digest?", "How would you log a user out of a stateless token system?"],
  revision: ["Authn = who; authz = what.", "Passwords: salted slow hash.", "JWT = header.payload.signature (HS256 = HMAC).", "Verify signature, algorithm and exp; short lifetimes."],
  hinglish: "Authentication batata hai tum kaun ho aur authorisation batata hai tum kya kar sakte ho. Password kabhi seedha store mat karo, salt ke saath dheema hash (argon2, bcrypt, scrypt) rakho. JWT ka payload encrypted nahi hota, sirf signed hota hai, isliye usme secret mat daalo.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("How should passwords be stored?", ["Plain text", "Salted slow hash", "MD5", "Base64"], 1, "A salted, slow hash such as argon2 or scrypt."),
    mcq("Is a JWT payload encrypted?", ["Yes, always", "No, it is only encoded and signed", "Only with HS256", "Only with HTTPS"], 1, "Anyone can read it; the signature protects integrity."),
    mcq("Which status means 'authenticated but not allowed'?", ["401", "403", "404", "422"], 1, "403 Forbidden."),
    mcq("What does the JWT signature prove?", ["The payload is secret", "The token was not modified and was made with the secret", "The user is an admin", "The token never expires"], 1, "Integrity and origin."),
    mcq("Why compare hashes with hmac.compare_digest?", ["It is faster", "It avoids timing attacks", "It is shorter", "It sorts"], 1, "Constant-time comparison.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Create a JWT", "Read a secret on the first line and a JSON object (the payload) on the second line. Print the HS256 JWT: header {\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"} and the payload both as compact JSON (separators , and :) in the key order given, base64url-encoded without padding, signed with HMAC-SHA256 using the secret over header.payload.", "import base64, hashlib, hmac, json\nsecret = input()\npayload = json.loads(input())\n# build and print the token\n", `import base64, hashlib, hmac, json\nsecret = input()\npayload = json.loads(input())\ndef b64(data):\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).rstrip(b"=").decode()\nheader = b64(json.dumps({"alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT"}, separators=(",", ":")).encode())\nbody = b64(json.dumps(payload, separators=(",", ":")).encode())\nsignature = hmac.new(secret.encode(), f"{header}.{body}".encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()\nprint(f"{header}.{body}.{b64(signature)}")`, [["secret\n{\"sub\": \"1234567890\", \"name\": \"John Doe\", \"iat\": 1516239022}", "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjM0NTY3ODkwIiwibmFtZSI6IkpvaG4gRG9lIiwiaWF0IjoxNTE2MjM5MDIyfQ.XbPfbIHMI6arZ3Y922BhjWgQzWXcXNrz0ogtVhfEd2o"], ["k\n{\"a\": 1}", "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJhIjoxfQ.HYfzqBpLbCOo950TeHdytWxg8UHtAmEQCW7ITVky6Gc"]], [["secret\n{\"sub\": \"1234567890\", \"name\": \"John Doe\", \"iat\": 1516239022}", "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjM0NTY3ODkwIiwibmFtZSI6IkpvaG4gRG9lIiwiaWF0IjoxNTE2MjM5MDIyfQ.XbPfbIHMI6arZ3Y922BhjWgQzWXcXNrz0ogtVhfEd2o"], ["k\n{\"a\": 1}", "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJhIjoxfQ.HYfzqBpLbCOo950TeHdytWxg8UHtAmEQCW7ITVky6Gc"], ["s3cret\n{\"sub\": \"asha\", \"role\": \"student\", \"exp\": 2000}", "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhc2hhIiwicm9sZSI6InN0dWRlbnQiLCJleHAiOjIwMDB9.rn09IKnL-6LA9Z7N1LQcaeOeESnvcOnPANbXJNskP2c"], ["pw\n{\"user\": \"ravi\", \"admin\": false}", "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJ1c2VyIjoicmF2aSIsImFkbWluIjpmYWxzZX0.mEuAU0XK0W0_28ZicUVXuLxvFfc1CPRrAeIK63QQafc"]])],
});

